{"schema":1,"slug":"shawnee-kentucky-ohio-river-and-towns","nation":"Shawnee","baseRef":"/data/web/territories/_base-ohio-valley.json","yearStart":1715,"yearEnd":1795,"presenceLabel":"Shawnee towns, sites or treaty line in this area","presenceNote":"c.1715 is the founding of Eskippakithiki; 1795 is the Treaty of Greenville. The Shawnee claim inside Kentucky is UNKNOWN (no source gives a boundary) and is NOT drawn. Ohio towns are shown as a county footprint, not a boundary.","confidence":"low-medium","approximate":true,"overlapNote":"Overlaps Cherokee (treaty lines 1768–1775), the Six Nations (who ceded Kentucky to the Crown at Fort Stanwix 1768 over Shawnee objection), Chickasaw (far west) and Delaware, Mingo, Wyandot and Miami in Ohio. Sources disagree on which Kentucky claim was strongest (see the Cherokee file). The Ohio River line of 1774 is a colonial understanding, and its terms are not preserved.","basis":"Hand-simplified from Greenville (1795) and Fort Finney (1786) treaty text, secondary accounts of Camp Charlotte (1774), and site evidence (Lower Shawneetown, Eskippakithiki). Native Land Digital data was not used.","basisChip":"mixed","timeline":{"min":1715,"max":1800},"fadeSouth":false,"view":{"full":"74 47 888 705","narrow":"74 47 888 705","card":"74 47 888 705"},"mapLabelPos":[-82.6,41.0],"pins":[],"startEra":{"treaty-of-camp-charlotte-1774":"camp","shawnee-chillicothe-divisions":"camp"},"stories":{"treaty-of-camp-charlotte-1774":{"yearStart":1774,"yearEnd":1795,"presenceLabel":"Shawnee Ohio River line and towns in this area"},"shawnee-chillicothe-divisions":{"yearStart":1774,"yearEnd":1780,"presenceLabel":"Old Chillicothe (Shawnee) in this area"}},"legend":[{"kind":"line","label":"Ohio River line (1774) / Greenville line (1795)"},{"kind":"hatch","label":"Ceded (Greenville 1795)"},{"kind":"soft","label":"Town area (county footprint, not a boundary)"},{"kind":"point","label":"Site"},{"kind":"ring","label":"Site, location approximate"}],"eras":[{"id":"pre","label":"c.1715–1774 · Documented sites; Kentucky claim unknown","short":"1715","yearStart":1715,"yearEnd":1774,"confidence":"low-medium","sources":["ewv_shawnee","hend92","lower_ksa","eski_ekh","weemeyakwe"],"storySlugs":[],"notDrawn":{"map":"SHAWNEE CLAIM IN KENTUCKY: UNKNOWN · NOT DRAWN","tag":"Kentucky claim: unknown, not drawn"},"text":"The West Virginia Encyclopedia says the Shawnee claimed most of Kentucky, but no source gives a boundary, so the map draws NO Shawnee area in Kentucky: the extent is unknown. Two dated Shawnee sites are documented in or beside Kentucky: Lower Shawneetown at the Scioto mouth (mid-1730s to 1758, about 1,200 people in 1751) and Eskippakithiki in Clark County (c.1715/1718 to c.1754). Main villages and summer camps were north of the Ohio.","layers":[{"feature":"s_lower","style":"point"},{"feature":"s_eski","style":"point"}],"mapLabel":"SHAWNEE","mapLabelPos":[-82.6,41.0]},{"id":"camp","label":"1774–1795 · Camp Charlotte: the Ohio River","short":"1774","yearStart":1774,"yearEnd":1795,"confidence":"low-medium","sources":["wp_camp","ewv_camp","wp_dunmore","battlefields","chalah","hend92","loganhist","finney"],"storySlugs":["treaty-of-camp-charlotte-1774","shawnee-chillicothe-divisions"],"notDrawn":{"map":"SHAWNEE CLAIM IN KENTUCKY: UNKNOWN · NOT DRAWN","tag":"Kentucky claim: unknown, not drawn"},"text":"After Lord Dunmore’s War, the Shawnee agreed at Camp Charlotte on Scippo Creek, Pickaway County, Ohio (19 Oct 1774) to stop hunting south of the Ohio and to recognise the river as the boundary. No copy of the treaty survives, so the terms are known only from accounts. The line drawn is the Ohio River from the Kanawha mouth to Cairo; the west end is our choice, not a source’s. The Shawnee moved their towns to the Scioto, Little Miami, Mad River and Great Miami valleys; Old Chillicothe near Xenia lasted 1774–80. The dashed counties are a town footprint, not a boundary. The 1786 Treaty of Fort Finney describes land partly by an unlocated river, so it is not drawn.","layers":[{"feature":"ohio_towns","style":"soft"},{"feature":"ohio_line","style":"line"},{"feature":"s_oldchill","style":"point"}],"mapLabel":"SHAWNEE","mapLabelPos":[-82.6,41.0]},{"id":"gv","label":"1795 → · Treaty of Greenville","short":"1795","yearStart":1795,"confidence":"medium","sources":["greenville"],"storySlugs":[],"notDrawn":{"map":"SHAWNEE CLAIM IN KENTUCKY: UNKNOWN · NOT DRAWN","tag":"Kentucky claim: unknown, not drawn"},"text":"On 3 Aug 1795 the tribes at Greenville ceded most of Ohio and a strip of Indiana east and south of a line from the Cuyahoga mouth via Fort Loramie and Fort Recovery to the Ohio opposite the mouth of the Kentucky River. Article 7 allowed hunting on ceded land. The line follows the treaty’s calls; the hatched area is closed by hand along the Ohio River and Lake Erie.","layers":[{"feature":"gv_ceded","style":"hatch"},{"feature":"gv_line","style":"line"}],"mapLabel":"CEDED 1795","mapLabelPos":[-82.6,41.0]}],"sources":[{"id":"wp_camp","label":"Treaty of Camp Charlotte (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Camp_Charlotte"},{"id":"wp_dunmore","label":"Lord Dunmore's War (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_Dunmore%27s_War"},{"id":"ewv_camp","label":"Treaty of Camp Charlotte, e-WV","url":"https://wvencyclopedia.org/entries/732"},{"id":"ewv_shawnee","label":"Shawnee, e-WV","url":"https://wvencyclopedia.org/entries/356"},{"id":"battlefields","label":"Proving Ground: Revolutionary Ohio (American Battlefield Trust)","url":"https://www.battlefields.org/learn/articles/proving-ground-revolutionary-ohio"},{"id":"chalah","label":"Chalahgawtha (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalahgawtha"},{"id":"lower_ksa","label":"Lower Shawneetown, Kentucky Archaeological Survey","url":"https://archaeology.ky.gov/Find-a-Site/Pages/Lower-Shawneetown.aspx"},{"id":"weemeyakwe","label":"Weemeyakwe (Scioto River), Shawnee Tribe","url":"https://shawnee-nsn.gov/news/weemeyakwe-scioto-river/"},{"id":"eski_ekh","label":"Indian Old Fields, ExploreKYHistory","url":"https://explorekyhistory.ky.gov/items/show/596"},{"id":"hend92","label":"Henderson, \"Dispelling the Myth\", Register of the KHS 90(1)","url":"https://preservationkentucky.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Dispelling-the-Myth-17th-18th-Century-Indian-Life-in-KY.pdf"},{"id":"finney","label":"Treaty of Fort Finney, 31 Jan 1786 (Avalon Project)","url":"https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/shaw1786.asp"},{"id":"greenville","label":"Treaty of Greenville, 3 Aug 1795 (Avalon Project)","url":"https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/greenvil.asp"},{"id":"loganhist","label":"Logan County Indian history (Mad River towns)","url":"https://www.loganhistory.org/indian-history"}],"geojson":{"type":"FeatureCollection","features":[{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"ohio_line","kind":"1774 understanding (terms not preserved)","note":"Ohio River from the Kanawha mouth to Cairo, following Natural Earth centreline. The treaty terms are not preserved; sources say the Shawnee recognised the Ohio as boundary and would not hunt south of it. The west end (Cairo) is my choice, not a source call.","approximate":true},"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-82.1431,38.8603],[-82.1434,38.8582],[-82.1483,38.8489],[-82.1613,38.8313],[-82.1729,38.8082],[-82.1919,38.7979],[-82.2089,38.7859],[-82.2117,38.7625],[-82.2045,38.7453],[-82.1954,38.7316],[-82.1876,38.7152],[-82.1844,38.69],[-82.1908,38.6756],[-82.1925,38.6654],[-82.1833,38.648],[-82.1792,38.6353],[-82.1769,38.6218],[-82.1776,38.6117],[-82.1929,38.5952],[-82.2152,38.5919],[-82.2633,38.5974],[-82.2772,38.5882],[-82.2883,38.5666],[-82.3096,38.4786],[-82.317,38.4609],[-82.3319,38.4435],[-82.3497,38.4379],[-82.4246,38.4329],[-82.5091,38.409],[-82.5528,38.4055],[-82.5891,38.4187],[-82.5996,38.4312],[-82.616,38.4634],[-82.6263,38.4776],[-82.6983,38.5353],[-82.7367,38.5544],[-82.7833,38.5672],[-82.8267,38.5864],[-82.8559,38.6248],[-82.8802,38.691],[-82.8833,